course: dictionary
lesson: 5102
title: Inside or within
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Internally is an adverb, so it tells you where or how something happens. It means inside something, such as a body, a building or a machine, or within a group, not involving people outside it. «"Internally displaced person" means someone forced from home who is still inside their own country.» Here internally means inside the country.
explain: The clues around the word show which meaning fits. «The school filled the job internally, so only its own teachers could apply.» The words only its own teachers tell you that outsiders were left out, so internally means within the school. Ask yourself: would "inside" still make sense in the sentence? Here it does.
explain: The same word can mean different things. «The medicine is not to be taken internally» means it must not be swallowed. «The injury was bleeding internally» means blood is leaking inside the body, which is very serious and needs a doctor at once. The context decides which meaning is right.
fact: Internally means inside something, or within a group.
fact: Internally is an adverb, so it describes where or how something happens.
fact: «Internally displaced person» means someone forced from home who is still in their own country.
fact: «Bleeding internally» means bleeding inside the body, which is very serious.
fact: «Dealt with internally» means handled by people inside the organisation, not by outsiders.
exercise: «The company will fill the manager job internally.» What does internally mean here? | By people from outside the company | *Only by people inside the company | Loudly, so everyone can hear | Out in the open for everyone to see
exercise: «The doctor said the injury was bleeding internally.» Where is the bleeding? | On the skin, where everyone can see | *Inside the body | Out of the window | In the hospital car park
exercise: «An internally displaced person is still inside their own country.» Find and copy one word from this sentence that means inside. | person | country | *internally | forced
test: «The shop dealt with the complaint internally, without telling the newspapers.» What does internally mean? | Quickly, by post | *Within the shop, not involving outsiders | Loudly and in public | Inside the newspaper
test: «The engine works internally, so you cannot see its parts.» Where do the parts work? | In the sky | *Inside the engine | Under the road | On the outside of the engine
exam: «The school will choose a new head teacher internally, so only its staff can apply.» What does internally tell you about who can apply? | Anyone in the world | Only children | *Only people already working at the school | Only people who live nearby
